  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1124 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nSurface analysis as of 9 PM shows remnants of a cut-off low over\nnorthern Georgia. Weak ridging continues to impinge on the Mid-\nSouth which kept skies clear and allowed temperatures to warm to\nseasonal temperatures earlier in the day. Skies should remain\nmostly clear overnight, with light to calm winds, and will cool\nnicely overnight into Thursday morning with temperatures in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to expand through the coming\ndays and continue to raise temperatures to the low to mid 90s for\nthe next several days. Southerly flow will increase as well, and\nbring an influx of moisture and raise humidity levels. Triple\ndigit heat indices are likely to return to the Delta region\ntomorrow and continue to spread geographically through the\nweekend. As moisture advection increases, heat headlines are not\nout of the question. A Heat Advisory could be needed as early as\nFriday in the Delta, but will continue to monitor as hi-res\nguidance comes into view.\n\nWeak troughing will sweep across the Great Lakes this weekend.\nResulting cloud cover may temper temperatures and heat indices\nslightly, though exact impacts remain uncertain. The exact path\nof this trough is still uncertain, but a few showers could\ndevelop Friday night into Saturday. A large area of high pressure\nis anticipated to build and encompass most of the southeast\nbeginning Sunday. Hot and humid conditions will continue under\nthis regime with diurnally driven thunderstorms likely each\nafternoon. While the region is no stranger to hot and humid\nweather, be sure to hydrate early and practice heat safety\nguidelines as we return to summer-like temperatures.\n\nDNM",
